  "strategy": "We are going to implement a novel approach that uses constrained optimization and symmetry exploitation to minimize the peak autocorrelation. The previous program used Adam optimization and coordinate descent but may have gotten stuck in local optima. We introduce a new method that parametrizes step-function-like sequences and optimizes them over a fundamental period.\n\nOur strategy:\n1. Generate candidate sequences using a parametric model: we create sequences that are built from two identical halves (or nearly identical for odd lengths)\n2. Use the first half as a polynomial (exponential to avoid early integer issues) of the length (exp(-np.pi*ptrange))\n3. Optimize a fixed-length candidate first, then with periodic doubling to expand length while preserving structure\n4. Use projected gradient methods to enforce periodicity and symmetry constraints during optimization\n5. Verify and polish solutions with the base optimization method\n\nThis new approach is different from the previous ones by:\n- Exploiting periodicity and symmetry to reduce dimensionality\n- Using exponential parametrizations for better scaling at large values\n- Coordinated length expansion based on optimizer progress\n\nAlgorithmic details:\nWe define two types of base sequences (for even and odd lengths) and then use projected gradient optimization (quasi-Newton method) with two distinct stages:\n\nStage 1: Base Optimization\n  - For a sequence length n, generate a base sequence via parametric expansion\n    \u2022 b = exp(-pi * x) where x runs over the half-length indices\n  - Project this onto the simplex and apply quasi-Newton optimization\n\nStage 2: Progressive Period Doubling\n  - Monitor progress (autocorrelation value) and consider period doubling when:\n    \u2022 Optimization stabilizes at a new minimum\n    \u2022 Peak reduction grows smaller than 0.0001 relative to current value\n  - When doubling, we:\n    \u2022 Take our candidate solution\n    \u2022 Expand it to satisfy new constraints by duplicating and adjusting\n    \u2022 Run the same optimization routine on the new double-length candidate\n\nImplementation:\nWe'll use a stateful optimization method that tracks:\n- Sequence length\n- Gradient steps\n- Value history\n- Optimization progress\n\nPeriod doubling is chosen because it preserves the essential behavior while expanding the parameter space. The exponential basis ensures we have solutions for large values without numerical instability.\n\nThe code must include:\n- Symmetric sequence generation\n- Period doubling expansion\n- Projected gradient optimization with symmetry and periodicity constraints\n- Early termination checks for complete optimization\n- Fallback if no explicit lower bound found (must return a valid sequence)\n\nWe'll also include aggressive random restarts at each step to avoid local minima. The symmetric expansion should help by ensuring no negative \"interference\" results.",
